| # GNU Make searches the directory list for a name it cannot find on disk before
| # it looks for an implicit rule — `f_mtime` runs above `try_implicit_rule` in
| # `update_file_1` (remake.c), and its own comment says so: looking at the modtime
| # beforehand means the file "may not need an implicit rule". When the search
| # lands on a path the makefile declares a target for, `rehash_file` (file.c)
| # makes the two names one file, retaining the object at the found path.
| #
| # So `foo.x` is `vpa/foo.x`, and it is built by the recipe written for that name
| # rather than by the pattern rule that matches the name as written; `bar.x` is
| # `vpa/bar.x`, whose rule carries a prerequisite and no recipe, so the pattern
| # rule does apply there and is handed `dep`.
| #
| # Each recipe writes its own file rather than appending to a shared one, so
| # what the build wrote does not depend on the order two independent recipes ran
| # in.
| VPATH = vpa
| 
| all: foo.x bar.x ; @printf 'all [$^]\n' > all.out
| 
| %.x: ; @printf 'pattern $@ [$^]\n' > pattern.out
| 
| vpa/foo.x: ; @printf 'vpath $@\n' > vpath.out
| 
| dep: ; @printf 'dep\n' > dep.out
| 
| vpa/bar.x: dep
file all.out touched 1
| all [vpa/foo.x vpa/bar.x]
file dep.out touched 1
| dep
file pattern.out touched 1
| pattern vpa/bar.x [dep]
file vpath.out touched 1
| vpath vpa/foo.x
